WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:01.890
Yeah, and I think he has a -

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.690
Tom, Tom.

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.790
- that he's using to ... anyway.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:11.469
I love you, but everything you've described
is the stuff I read obsessively.

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:13.519
Hello.

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:18.900
So today we are going to talk about the DC
comic series that we most want to see turn

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.850
into a TV show.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.620
I am here with Tom and Nina to talk about
this.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:32.410
And my pick is a fairly obscure comic book
that I am 99% certain neither of you have

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:36.539
ever heard of, and it is called Major Bummer.

00:00:36.539 --> 00:00:39.890
Anyone, anyone?

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:43.170
No, you're correct that I'm not familiar.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:47.679
Now, the reason I'm sure of this is because
it was canceled after 15 issues.

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:48.819
Oh, okay.

00:00:48.819 --> 00:00:50.030
It came out in 1997.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.030
That tracks.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.030
Yeah.

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:56.659
And it lasted for a little over a year, but
the pedigree is solid.

00:00:56.659 --> 00:01:03.739
It was written by John Arcudi and penciled
by Doug Monk, who are the duo behind The Mask,

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:06.590
the original Mask comics that the movie was
based on.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:07.590
Wow.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:12.720
So they're real deal comic book big deal guys.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.620
So these aliens are in college, and part of
their thesis project is to give random people

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:23.150
superpowers and see what happens, only they
research the people and they -

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:24.800
That's weird, I had the same thesis project.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:25.800
Oh, really?

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.800
Yeah.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:27.800
How'd you do?

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:28.800
Not great.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.800
Not great.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.050
I think Nina and I just made the same face
the whole time that you were describing that.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.450
Just very, very concerned, yeah.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.450
It's a comedy.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.850
It's not a serious comic book.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:39.850
Yeah.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.840
It is filled with jokes and gags and hilarity.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:42.840
You're kidding?

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.310
No, I know it's hard to believe.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:48.371
So one of the aliens who's sending out the
superpower machines doesn't realize that the

00:01:48.371 --> 00:01:52.350
phone book lists names backwards, so sends
it to the wrong person.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:58.020
This guy, this slacker, Lou Martin, gets super
powers and gets super duper buff.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.440
And throughout the entire run of the comic
book series, which again, only lasts 15 issues,

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:07.920
he's basically completely uninterested in
doing anything heroic, anything at all.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.660
There's a super team that wants him to join
and he's just like, "Yeah, no, thanks.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:15.280
I would really rather play video games and
eat a sandwich."

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.280
So he's Ralph Bohner?

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.540
He's Ralph Bohner, exactly.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.230
Don't speak that name in this house.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.230
I'm sorry.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.790
I'm sorry to bring up such an unpleasant memory
for you.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:33.030
Major Bummer was 1997, and it's got very heavy
generation X vibes of what if a slacker got

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:34.030
super powers?

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:40.960
And I know that it's kind of a silly, lame
sounding logline, but at the end of the day,

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:46.990
giving irresponsible people superpowers is
probably one of the most used tropes in all

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:49.860
of these superhero movies and TV shows that
we actually really like.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.540
So if they kind of committed to it and went
all in on it and just had a pretty decent

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.819
budget, it could be really, really fun.

00:02:56.819 --> 00:02:57.819
I could dig that, yeah.

00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:00.900
I think it would be really great.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:06.890
I don't have any hope for it, because again,
15 issues, no one remembers it, but maybe

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:07.890
me.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:12.860
And then beyond that, there's not a super
great track record for comic book shows and

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.220
movies ... comic book shows specifically that
are specifically humorous.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:23.780
So The Tick, The Tick has had tons of TV shows
and they've all gotten canceled too early.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:24.930
And The Tick is amazing.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:25.930
I love The Tick.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:31.130
And it's just one of those things where for
some reason you can have comedy in your superheroes,

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.380
but you can't have your superheroes be a comedy.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.990
So that's my pick and I'm not holding out
any hope that it'll ever happen.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:39.730
I like it though.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:40.730
And I think you've -

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:41.730
I love it.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:42.730
- sold it well, where it's -

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:43.730
Thank you, Tom.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:44.730
Yeah, make it funny.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:45.730
Have some levity about this.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:49.569
I mean, that's why people like Marvel movies
in the first place, is because they had some

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:51.240
levity to them.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.890
Have your hero never learn anything and never
want to learn anything.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:55.890
Right.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:56.890
That's the premise.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:57.890
I love it.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:58.890
And it's wonderful.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:03:59.890
It's like if Dan Harmon wrote an MCU story.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:01.440
No, it's got all the component parts of a
Dan Harmon movie.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.379
It's got the flawed hero, just minus the learning.

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:08.989
If you got the right people involved, it could
be really amazing.

00:04:08.989 --> 00:04:09.989
Yeah.

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:14.430
And you mentioned Dan Harmon, and there's
definitely a lot going on in Rick and Morty

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.990
that I think probably ... again, I don't think
it's a direct line, but I would say that maybe

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:23.350
it's in the air, maybe somehow the things
that influence Major Bummer also then went

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:25.750
on to influence Rick and Morty in the same
way.

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:26.750
Got you.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:27.800
It just was ahead of its time.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.300
It was a beautiful flower that nobody but
me decided to smell.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:37.570
Years and years later ... my collection only
had issues two through 15 and years later,

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:41.690
I bought issue number one on eBay for a few
bucks.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:44.660
And I was like, "Finally, it's mine!"

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.139
And meanwhile, it was an $8 purchase.

00:04:46.139 --> 00:04:48.690
It's not rare, it's just that nobody -

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:49.690
Right.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:50.690
No one cares.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:51.690
Right.

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:52.690
But it's great.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:53.690
Those were always my favorite comics.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:56.650
I had every Alpha Flight, for whatever it's
worth.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.949
You are weirdly obsessed with Alpha Flight.

00:04:59.949 --> 00:05:00.949
You know what?

00:05:00.949 --> 00:05:04.890
If you ever want to do a full, let's say three
and a half hour video essay on Alpha Flight,

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:06.130
I am there for it.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.440
I'm a hundred percent down.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.250
Can we put a little name tag under him that
says Tom, weirdly obsessed with Alpha Flight,

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:12.610
for the remainder of the video.

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:14.340
I'd like that very much.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.340
Okay.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:16.340
Now everyone knows.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:17.340
Yeah.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.020
They spelled my name wrong.

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:27.630
Nina, what's your pick for the DC comic story
that you want to see turn into a TV show?

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:32.540
I typically prefer Marvel because of the aforementioned levity and comedy that they go for,

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:33.729
moreso than DC.

00:05:33.729 --> 00:05:37.639
I mean, Shazam was one of the more fun DC
movies.

00:05:37.639 --> 00:05:39.860
I thought Birds of Prey was a lot of fun,
stuff like that.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:42.830
But I also love the HBO adaptation.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:47.190
I know Watchmen kind of ... Watchmen is DC,
yes?

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:51.660
Watchmen is DC, and now it's more DC than
it ever was.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:52.660
Yeah.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:53.660
Because for a long time -

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:54.660
I feel like ... yeah.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.120
Yeah, for a long time it was separate and
apart from DC.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.120
It was -

00:05:58.120 --> 00:05:59.949
That's what I thought.

00:05:59.949 --> 00:06:01.440
Brief Watchmen history lesson.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:02.440
Yes, please.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:08.270
All the Watchmen characters are based on Charlton
Comics characters that DC had acquired and

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:09.389
planned to use.

00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:10.389
Alan Moore wanted -

00:06:10.389 --> 00:06:11.610
He knows his stuff.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:16.280
Alan Moore wanted to use all of these characters
and DC said, "No, we have plans for Blue Beetle.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:18.160
Make him Night Owl instead," so he did.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.470
This is why we pay you the big bucks, Brian.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:22.470
I know some things.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:28.670
So I loved the HBO adaptation of Watchmen,
as I suspect we all did, and pretty much everybody

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:29.670
did.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.770
So I kind of went hunting for something else
that I thought could make a cool mini series

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:34.200
that was a little on the darker side.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:40.050
And I came across the 1996 series written
by Mark Wade and Alex Ross called Kingdom

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.050
Come.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:42.780
Are you two familiar?

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:43.870
Kingdom Come!

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:44.870
Yeah.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:45.870
That's so good.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:46.870
Yeah.

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.270
Oh, I'm so mad I didn't think of that.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:50.630
That's real bleak.

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:53.539
I read a good portion of it.

00:06:53.539 --> 00:06:56.070
It's also a beautiful comic.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:57.490
Alex Ross, baby.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:01.870
I know that you've both seen it, but I'm sending
a panel from it anyway.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.870
It's just ... it was stunning to look at.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:09.479
I kind of got really wrapped up in reading
it, to the detriment of my work, probably.

00:07:09.479 --> 00:07:14.930
It felt very current as well, because The
Boys has been such a big hit as well, in kind

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:20.720
of examining the role of superheroes and whether
or not we can just blindly put our trust in

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:25.990
them or if they're taking advantage of the
public good will.

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:29.850
I would say that The Boys owes almost everything
to Kingdom Come.

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:30.850
Exactly.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:33.509
So I think this would be a really good move
for DC to take.

00:07:33.509 --> 00:07:37.479
The OG Justice League ... bring the same actors
back, that's fine.

00:07:37.479 --> 00:07:42.720
And then cast that ragtag group of miscreants
who just run around murdering everybody.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:49.419
So just to briefly recap what the premise
of Kingdom Come is, basically it's the future

00:07:49.419 --> 00:07:54.310
and superheroes just don't care about kind
of collateral damage.

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:58.690
They're just super power beings and the fighting
all the time and they call themselves superheroes,

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:01.060
but they're mostly just super powered.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:04.720
And they're led by Magog, who will just murder
with impunity.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:09.900
And everyone's rallying behind him, which
the Justice League is like, "Why?

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.250
Why is this acceptable?"

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:14.990
Right, but most of the Justice League is retired
by this point.

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:15.990
Right.

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:21.300
I think Superman's living on a farm and Diana's
back ... yeah, she's back -

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:22.300
She's on.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:24.669
Thank you for pronouncing it.

00:08:24.669 --> 00:08:29.740
Yeah, so they've scattered to their ends of
the earth, and then this -

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:36.070
It's kind of this beautiful retirement story
for a bunch of, just existing too fast.

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:42.940
It also felt very kind of modern, even though
it was written in the nineties, where during

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:48.720
a fight with two superheroes, one of them
gets ... I believe it's Captain Atom gets

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.930
yanked apart and it gives the entire American
Midwest radiation poisoning.

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:54.930
Yeah.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:58.600
Which is funny when you say it, and then it's
also so upsetting.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:00.250
And that's the reason that they all leave,
right?

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:01.250
Right.

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:02.250
So yeah.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:05.770
I mean Brian, you probably have much more
to explain about this than my ... I've read

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:07.940
about half the issues, but -

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:12.560
What's great about it is it's basically this
story of what does it mean to be a hero?

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:13.820
What does it mean to have power?

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:19.620
And it's Superman ... basically Batman kind
of wrangles Superman and other people out

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:22.420
of retirement, if I'm remembering all the
details correctly.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:24.380
I believe that's correct, yeah.

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:30.300
And basically the idea is just kind of the
older heroes kind of coming after the newer

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:32.110
heroes to try to say, "Hey, stop it.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:34.400
You can't do this.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:37.760
You're tearing the world apart, and here's
why, and here's how."

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:39.450
It's such a pretty story.

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:43.450
We got Shazam, Captain Marvel ... back when
they could call him Captain Marvel still -

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:44.589
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:44.589 --> 00:09:45.589
- fighting Superman.

00:09:45.589 --> 00:09:48.910
And that fight scene is honestly one of the
most exciting.

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:50.540
It's so ... you haven't gotten there yet,
Nina.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:51.540
The way they drew that.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:52.540
You got to get to the end.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:53.540
You got to finish this thing.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:54.540
It's so good.

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:55.540
Yeah, I'm going to.

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:56.540
Oh my God, what a pick.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:58.270
You know what I'm going to do after the call
today?

00:09:58.270 --> 00:09:59.570
I'm going to read Kingdom Come.

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:00.930
It sounds super exciting.

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:01.930
I love it.

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:02.930
I've inspired you.

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:03.930
It's such a great story.

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:04.930
Yeah.

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:05.930
It's such a great pick.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.360
And I just think on a network like HBO, that
could give it a budget and a really good director,

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:10.560
it could be amazing.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:11.560
It would be amazing.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:15.529
And I feel like HBO is the perfect place,
because I feel like they wouldn't over-bloat

00:10:15.529 --> 00:10:18.610
the story and make it and be 13 or 14 episodes.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:22.540
They'd let it be five, maybe six.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:27.720
But yeah, I want a dark Kingdom Come adaptation.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:29.180
Yeah.

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:31.140
But it's ultimately a story of hope.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:32.140
Exactly.

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:33.140
And that's the thing.

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:34.140
Yeah.

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:36.880
Because it's all about the value of having
values.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.360
Oh, I feel like such a cheesy corny loser,
but yeah.

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:43.540
No, and the problem is I just want Damon Lindelof
to direct it too, but I also understand he

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:44.840
can't do everything.

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:49.680
He can't do everything, but he's definitely
proven himself with Watchmen.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:55.320
When that Watchmen show was announced, I looked
at the trailer and I was like, "What?

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:56.430
Really?

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:57.500
Are you sure?"

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:58.500
Yeah.

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:03.120
Because I am not a big fan of the Watchmen
movie from our friend Zach Snyder.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.339
I wasn't aware that anyone was, but yeah,
go on.

00:11:05.339 --> 00:11:09.930
There are people who like that movie, and
I will not say that they are wrong to like

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:10.930
it.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:11.930
You're right.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:13.290
Everyone is allowed to feel that way.

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:15.089
I will say, it's not for me.

00:11:15.089 --> 00:11:16.089
It's not for me.

00:11:16.089 --> 00:11:22.380
And maybe some other day we'll talk about
why, but Damon Lindelof definitely did an

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:26.420
amazing job with Watchmen and he put all my
fears to rest.

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:28.970
So yeah, that's a great pick, Nina.

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:29.970
Thank you.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:30.970
I impressed the comics king.

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:31.970
I'm retired.

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:33.140
It's a great idea.

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:34.560
Goodbye everyone.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:35.980
Bye, Nina.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:38.120
I'm going to George Costanza this.

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:39.390
I'm out.

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:44.470
Nina turned into star dust.

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:46.440
So Tom, what's your pick?

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.920
I present to you my pitch.

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:52.770
Consider for a moment the fact that by their
very nature, television adaptations ... and

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:55.320
keeping in mind that I love Watchmen.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:57.459
I felt Watchmen was terribly impressive.

00:11:57.459 --> 00:12:04.620
I also love a lot of the kind of lower budget,
really beautiful, brilliant TV adaptations

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:08.440
that we've seen in the last few years, like
Doom Patrol, I think is kind of gorgeous in

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:09.540
its own unique way.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.500
There's some great DC television content out
there.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:14.700
So I don't mean to disparage that.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:22.190
However, television adaptations do come with
sort of a pre-established trashiness, right?

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:23.190
Yes.

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:24.190
Yeah.

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:28.840
Either visually, in terms of the special effects,
or in terms of the writing, sometimes both.

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:29.840
Can we agree on that?

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:30.840
Yes.

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:31.840
I agree.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:33.440
I agree.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:37.930
That said, the last few years, television
has kind of shifted a little bit and turned

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:47.709
into, at least in some places, think pieces
and really clever narratives, like True Detective,

00:12:47.709 --> 00:12:55.700
and like Watchmen, and overall, just these
things that I'm not smart enough to understand.

00:12:55.700 --> 00:13:02.060
So I think that the best possible television
adaptation would be something that combines

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:07.730
these three things: terrible visuals, not
great writing, and I don't understand it,

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:15.410
which is why I think that the best TV DC adaptation
would be every comic that DC put out in the

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:20.790
nineties.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:25.240
So I've got a whole list of stuff that would
work perfectly for that.

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:35.140
So you've got red and blue Superman, both
of them looking sort of like butt, and I still

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:36.970
don't get what happened.

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:41.389
Superman dies and half of the panels are just
dedicated to trying to advertise these new

00:13:41.389 --> 00:13:47.100
characters that nobody cares about with names
that I don't even remember anymore.

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:54.590
Asriel taking over as Batman, Batman becomes
a French guy with too many spikes on his body.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:14:00.170
Then we get whatever Eradicator was, because
I'm, again, still not clear on that.

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:04.010
Aquaman loses a hand and it gets it back and
we don't understand why.

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:08.360
Nightwing has a mullet and then he cuts it
off.

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:12.660
Parallax, Dr. Fate in the nineties ... I don't
know if you guys remember this, but Dr. Fate

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:18.840
all of a sudden abandoned the Hippocratic
Oath and just turned into this ... I don't

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:20.820
know if you've ever read Grimjack -

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:21.820
Serious accusation, Tom.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:25.610
But he basically turned into Grimjack.

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:26.740
He's missing an eye and I think -

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:27.740
He has the ankh over his eye?

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:29.360
Yeah, and I think he has -

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:30.360
Tom -

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:33.889
- a knife that he's using to ... anyway.

00:14:33.889 --> 00:14:39.471
I love you, but everything you've described
is the stuff I read obsessively, and I love

00:14:39.471 --> 00:14:43.000
all of this and I can probably explain most
of it.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:48.160
All right, well let's stop recording and then
Brian's going to make me feel bad about it.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:49.160
No, I'm just saying -

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:51.050
If you don't understand something, you just
got to ask Brian.

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:53.850
I'm just saying ... okay, so I really like
this idea.

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:56.760
I really like the ... Superman red blue was
ridiculous.

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:02.779
It is a little hard to kind of work out what
exactly happened there, but it also was an

00:15:02.779 --> 00:15:07.560
homage to a storyline from the fifties where
Superman red and Superman blue happen.

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:12.160
And also, another DC comic book from the nineties,
Kingdom Come.

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:13.420
Very true.

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:15.100
I was going to say.

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:16.100
And also -

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:17.100
1996.

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:22.250
- all these JLA comics right here, the Grant
Morrison JLAs that I love.

00:15:22.250 --> 00:15:23.250
Yeah.

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:26.880
All these Starman comic books that are amazing.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:33.540
Grant Morrison has always been kind of a ... what
do you call it, lighthouse in a foggy ocean.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:37.019
But Starman was James Robinson.

00:15:37.019 --> 00:15:39.410
I stand corrected.

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:40.589
I'm sorry, Tom.

00:15:40.589 --> 00:15:42.170
No, I'm a fool.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:49.280
All I'm saying is that ... did you guys watch
the Crisis on Infinite Earth special that

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:50.740
they did with the Arrow-verse about two years
ago?

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:53.449
I did not, but I'm aware of it.

00:15:53.449 --> 00:15:55.180
Yeah, I've seen the name.

00:15:55.180 --> 00:16:00.690
It feels like if Terry Gilliam had sneezed
that movie into existence, kind of accidentally.

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:02.930
It's rough.

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:03.930
It's real rough.

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:04.930
That's bone chilling.

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:13.740
And I feel like the quality of that mixed
with the kind of absence of consciousness,

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:18.080
I feel like that could've made a wonderful
television series.

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:21.320
Just nothing makes sense and everything looks
like crap.

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:22.449
Here's what I think is happening.

00:16:22.449 --> 00:16:28.230
I think you're picking up on the fact that
DC comics, more than Marvel, has had a more

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:32.089
difficult relationship with it's continuity,
basically.

00:16:32.089 --> 00:16:33.089
Yeah.

00:16:33.089 --> 00:16:34.089
Sure.

00:16:34.089 --> 00:16:38.290
In the late eighties, the Crisis on Infinite
Earth, the thing that you mentioned, was basically

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:42.220
... so way back in the forties, we have Superman,
we have Batman, we have The Flash, we have

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:43.220
Green Lantern, we have Wonder Woman.

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:47.000
We have all these characters, and then they
go away for a while.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:51.000
And then in the late fifties ... I want to
say late fifties, early sixties, they bring

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.709
them back, a lot of them, but new versions
of them.

00:16:53.709 --> 00:16:57.529
You've got a new Flash, you've got a new Green
Lantern, you've got Wonder Woman, but started

00:16:57.529 --> 00:16:58.529
over.

00:16:58.529 --> 00:17:01.610
You've got new versions of old characters
names like The Atom.

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:07.720
In the Golden Age, The Atom was just a short
guy, but in the Silver Age, he could actually

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:08.720
shrink.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:09.929
Wait, is that real?

00:17:09.929 --> 00:17:10.929
Yes.

00:17:10.929 --> 00:17:12.419
Adam was just a small man?

00:17:12.419 --> 00:17:14.259
He was just really small.

00:17:14.259 --> 00:17:19.720
He had a blue mask that covered his whole
face and a cape and a weightlifting belt and

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:27.459
he just ran around punching Nazis and other
crooks and yegs and henchmen and things.

00:17:27.459 --> 00:17:33.409
So what happens is over time, DC comics has
all of these different continuities that is

00:17:33.409 --> 00:17:37.659
just confusing, so the Silver Age heroes are
these different versions of the Golden Age

00:17:37.659 --> 00:17:40.299
heroes and they said, "How do we reconcile
this?

00:17:40.299 --> 00:17:42.059
They live on parallel earths."

00:17:42.059 --> 00:17:46.229
So eventually you have earth one, earth two,
earth X, earth ... I don't know, it just goes

00:17:46.229 --> 00:17:47.400
on and on and on.

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:52.470
And then as DC comics acquires different comic
book characters, like Captain Marvel -

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:53.710
Captain Marvel.

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:57.220
- which was published by Faucet Comics.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:18:00.940
And then all the Charlton Comics characters
that we talked about earlier that were going

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:05.659
to be the Watchmen characters, they were all
existing on separate earths.

00:18:05.659 --> 00:18:10.379
So in order to clean it up and make it seem
a little bit more streamlined, they did Crisis

00:18:10.379 --> 00:18:15.489
on Infinite Earth, a bunch of characters died,
and eventually they were just like, "Okay,

00:18:15.489 --> 00:18:16.700
here's when everything started.

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:17.700
Everything's on the same earth.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:18.700
This is how it happened."

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:20.200
So that was just a house cleaning exercise.

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:21.559
That's all it was.

00:18:21.559 --> 00:18:22.600
And that's all it ever was.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:26.279
But they kept doing it every ten years for
the rest of time.

00:18:26.279 --> 00:18:27.879
They kept doing it, yes.

00:18:27.879 --> 00:18:33.179
And that's the problem, because then in the
nineties they did Zero Hour, and then a bunch

00:18:33.179 --> 00:18:39.429
of years later, they did the New 52, which
was when I ... shortly after the New 52, I

00:18:39.429 --> 00:18:42.479
hopped off, because I just couldn't keep up
anymore.

00:18:42.479 --> 00:18:45.369
But yeah, that's been more of DC's thing.

00:18:45.369 --> 00:18:50.789
Marvel hasn't done that quite as much, but
they have been doing it too, because our favorite

00:18:50.789 --> 00:18:55.909
character Miles Morales, he's an awesome addition
to the Spider-Man universe, he started out

00:18:55.909 --> 00:19:00.780
as the second ultimate Spiderman on the ultimate
universe and then he got folded into the main

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:05.419
line Marvel universe during the recent ... I
want to say Secret War.

00:19:05.419 --> 00:19:08.149
I think they recycled the old Secret War name.

00:19:08.149 --> 00:19:09.179
I think it was, yeah.

00:19:09.179 --> 00:19:14.090
I'm getting way off track here, but what you're
picking up on is the fact that in the nineties,

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:21.210
DC comics was very crazy and a lot of weird
stuff happened in an effort to try to do new

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:22.240
and interesting things.

00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:27.409
And they kept throwing weird ideas out, like
the death of Superman, the new Batman, John

00:19:27.409 --> 00:19:28.940
Paul.

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:31.320
John Paul, I don't remember ...

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:34.100
John Paul Valley was his name.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:37.270
And he was Asriel and he became Batman.

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:39.500
I think I'm babbling.

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:40.500
Not at all.

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:41.500
No.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:47.320
I think I mostly read comic books instead
of doing homework in high school.

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:49.919
And that's everything I've just -

00:19:49.919 --> 00:19:55.279
Well, to Brian's teachers watching this, now
you know.

00:19:55.279 --> 00:20:00.929
If Ms. from All Saints private school in the
fourth grade, I'm going to say about 1996

00:20:00.929 --> 00:20:06.139
or circa at that point, ever sees this ... nevermind,
I don't have anything poetic to say.

00:20:06.139 --> 00:20:08.029
Change my mind.

00:20:08.029 --> 00:20:09.029
Thanks for watching.

